{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And armed (chamushim) the children of Israel went up.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): Israel went up from Egypt girded like men of the army going forth to war. And even though Israel is not like the other nations, who need to arm themselves against enemies, it is the way of the Torah to command that a person should conduct himself partly in the way of nature and circumstance, and afterward the miracle will act. And so we find that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua (Joshua 8:2): ״שים לך אורב לעיר מאחריה״ — and together with the instruction for miracles and wonders, why would they need an ambush? Rather, it is the will of the Torah that a person should do in the way of nature everything within his power to do, and leave the rest in the hands of Heaven. And so Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 21:31): ״סוס מוכן ליום מלחמה ולה׳ התשועה״ — that is, human beings are obligated to guard their lives and prepare horses and weapons of war, and the Holy One, blessed be He, will deliver, for salvation belongs to Him alone, blessed be He. And there are those who interpreted וחמשים to mean that Israel went out, each one of them, with five weapons." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״וחמשים עלו״ — one out of five, and four parts died during the three days of darkness, so that the Egyptians would not see their downfall. And some say one out of fifty, and some say one out of five hundred; from here onward, go and calculate. ״ושלשים על כלו״ — some say three Egyptians went out against each and every one of Israel, and some say thirty, and some say three hundred. Thus far in the Midrash." "<b>And the explanation of the matter is</b> that the one who says one out of five — then Israel were initially five times sixty myriad, which is three hundred myriad. And his opinion is that ״ושלשים על כלו,״ in its explanation, is three hundred Egyptians for each and every one of Israel, which yields ninety thousand myriad of Egyptians who went out against them. And so we find in the Midrash: when the Holy One, blessed be He, descended upon Egypt, ninety thousand myriad angels of destruction descended upon the Egyptian camp. And the one who said one out of fifty — Israel were three thousand myriad, and his opinion is that ״ושלשים״ means thirty for each and every one; thus thirty times three thousand myriad is ninety thousand myriad. And the one who says one out of five hundred — Israel were initially thirty thousand myriad, and his opinion is that ״ושלשים״ means three for each and every one, and that is ninety thousand myriad. Thus the reckoning of all the sages converges, that the Egyptians who went out against them were ninety thousand myriad — whether according to the one who says one out of five, or one out of fifty, or one out of five hundred — together with the reckoning of ״ושלשים על כלו״ according to the opinion and reasoning of each of them. And corresponding to them, the Holy One, blessed be He, brought out against them ninety thousand angels of destruction. This is the explanation of this Midrash. And you will find that the word וחמשים is deficient — it is written חמשים without a vav — to teach you that the Torah, which was given at fifty days, was the ultimate purpose for which the children of Israel went up from the land of Egypt. And furthermore, it alludes to the fifty times that the Exodus from Egypt is mentioned in the Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:47.234954346Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}